<PageLayout
	title="About | Itziar Morales"
	description="Software Engineering student in Madrid — systems programming, backend, and data engineering. Also games, fantasy novels, and a home server."
>
	<p class="eyebrow">~/about</p>
	<h1>About</h1>

	<p>
		Hi, I'm Itziar, a Software Engineering student at <a href="https://www.u-tad.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U-tad</a> (Madrid)
		with a minor in Data Science &amp; AI, graduating in June 2027. I started programming at 18
		by enrolling at <a href="https://www.digipen.es" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DigiPen Bilbao</a> for
		a B.S. in Computer Science in Real Time Interactive Simulation, pivoted to Computational Mathematics,
		and landed on Software Engineering. Not the most direct path, but it left me with an unusually wide
		base &mdash; systems programming in C++, backend services, databases of every persuasion, and the
		Python data/ML stack. The full list, honestly self-rated, lives in <a href="/stack">~/stack</a>.
	</p>

	<p>
		Mathematics has been the constant thread: linear algebra for rendering pipelines, kinematics for
		collision detection, discrete maths for graph traversal, statistics for ML. Three years of
		Computational Mathematics turned that instinct into a foundation &mdash; I think about convergence
		and numerical stability as naturally as I think about runtime complexity.
	</p>

	<p>
		Outside of code I have a vocational degree in music (violoncello) and ABRSM Grade 8 in both piano
		and cello &mdash; which once turned into composing the soundtrack for a college game project.
	</p>

	<p>
		I use Linux as my daily driver, keep a self-hosted server, and tinker with dotfiles for window
		managers and Neovim more than I probably should. The full setup is in <a href="/uses">~/uses</a>.
	</p>

	<p>
		The rest of my free time splits fairly evenly between games and books (tallied in
		<a href="/log">~/log</a>). I bounce between retro titles and modern releases depending on the
		mood, and the games I've built have left me with a soft spot for good pixel art &mdash; plus a
		small pile of Python scripts for slicing tilesets and bundling spritesheets, because wrangling
		those assets by hand gets old fast. I read a lot of fantasy fiction, and I build my own PCs
		(and the family's, and the server's) &mdash; picking parts is half the fun.
	</p>

	<p>
		Languages are the other thread: English at C2 (Cambridge CPE), native Spanish, and intermediate
		French. Each one gives you a different lens on the world, and I find that genuinely enriching.
	</p>

	<p>
		I'm currently interning as a Data Analytics Intern at MGA (remote), where most of my time goes
		to the data-engineering side of a digital transformation: designing relational schemas, cleaning
		and migrating years of spreadsheet data into PostgreSQL, and building Spring Boot services on top.
		The part I enjoy most is turning implicit business rules &mdash; the kind that live in people's
		heads and formula cells &mdash; into constraints the database enforces itself.
	</p>

	<p>
		What I'm up to <em>right now</em> &mdash; this month's games, books, and piano included &mdash;
		lives in <a href="/now">~/now</a>. There's a <a href={CV_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">CV</a>
		if you want the concise version, and if any of this overlaps with something you're building, or you
		just have strong opinions about fantasy novels or window managers,
		<a href="mailto:itzimr97@pm.me">get in touch</a>.
	</p>
</PageLayout>
